# Make a first-level specialist mage, finish character generation, save him,
# and let INCURSION_CHAR_PROBE=1 write logs/charprobe.txt -- whose "Known
# Spells:" section is the oracle for the two specialty spells the class grant
# is supposed to give.
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat \
#   INCURSION_CHAR_PROBE=1 \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/specialty-grant-check.keys 42
#
# WHAT IS BEING MEASURED. The Mage class's EV_ADVANCE used to gain the two
# spells of the specialist's school from a switch in lib/classes.irh. That
# grant now lives in a * SPECIALTY_SPELLS list (school order 0-8, two spells
# each) read from src/Create.cpp. The two spells must still be known at level
# one, or the character sheet's Known Spells list loses them.
#
# WHY THE SCHOOL IS NAMED. @choose matches a menu entry by name (see @choose
# in src/Wposix.cpp), so adding a race, class or school cannot silently move
# the pick the way a counted letter would. The seed is fixed all the same:
# the attribute rolls decide which feats are offered, and the letters below
# for attributes and feats stay positional.
#
# WHY THIS ONE SAVES. The engine writes the character dump (DumpCharacter,
# src/Sheet.cpp) only from Game::SaveGame, under INCURSION_CHAR_PROBE=1
# (src/Registry.cpp:1105-1111). Saving is therefore how the check reads the
# Known Spells list; a session that only opened menus would write nothing and
# headless.sh would report NO GAMEPLAY.
#
# The school is Evocation, so the two expected spells are Magic Missile and
# Shield (lib/classes.irh SPECIALTY_SPELLS, school 4). The sibling file
# specialty-grant-check-nec.keys is the same script with Necromancy chosen.

a       # Initial Choices: [a] Create a New Character
n       # Alter the character generation options? -- no
y       # Your options will place the game in Explore Mode. Continue? -- yes
@choose "Orc"
@choose "Orc (Standard)"
@choose "Mage"
a       # Attributes: the first of the five offered sets
@choose "Neutral"   # Alignment
@choose "Evocation" # Wizard specialty
a       # First feat.  Which feats are offered depends on the attribute
a       # Second feat. rolls, so these two stay counted.

# Skill Manager. RIGHT spends a rank on the selected skill and stops at the
# skill's maximum, DOWN moves to the next, so three RIGHTs per skill spends
# everything available without needing to know how many ranks this character
# has. Character generation will not let go until the pool is empty.
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
ENTER y # Done, and confirm keeping the ranks

@while "wish to worship" y
@expect "personality archetype"
a       # Personality archetype

# The starting spells. A first-level mage is offered exactly three picks; the
# specialty spells are already known and are NOT on this list, so these picks
# are whatever the list opens on. @while rather than three counted a's.
@expect "Learn Spells: Level"    # the starting-spells checklist
SPACE DOWN SPACE DOWN SPACE DOWN SPACE DOWN    # mark the first rows; a full level refuses the rest
SPACE DOWN SPACE DOWN SPACE DOWN SPACE DOWN
ENTER
@until "Things in View" ESC

# One real turn, so this counts as a session and headless.sh does not report
# NO GAMEPLAY.
s
@dump:played

# Save. ESC opens the System Menu rather than cancelling anything, and [b] is
# Save and Continue: the save triggers the character probe.
ESC
@expect "System Menu"
b
@dump:saved
